**Constraints:**
- Cloud provider: GCP only (no AWS, Azure, or multi-cloud)
- Infrastructure as Code: Terraform (open-source CLI, not Terraform Cloud or Enterprise)
- Kubernetes packaging: Helm 3
- GitOps: ArgoCD with ApplicationSets
- Progressive delivery: Argo Rollouts for canary/blue-green deployments
- Service mesh: Istio
- CI/CD: GitHub Actions (self-hosted runners on GCP)
- Configuration management: Ansible for Instance Groups
- Budget: Optimize for cost; use shared resources with strong isolation controls
- Security & Compliance: SOC 2 Type II required; strict tenant isolation, least-privilege IAM, audit logging, VPC Service Controls, zero trust architecture
- Timeline: MVP architecture in 4 weeks, production-ready rollout in 12 weeks
- Team bandwidth: 5 engineers; automation-first approach is mandatory
- Data Cloning: GCP-native services only (Cloud SQL Clone, BigQuery Views/Scheduled Queries, Storage Transfer Service); no custom ETL scripts
- Avoid: Third-party paid IaC platforms (Spacelift, env0, Terraform Cloud), vendor lock-in tools, custom data sync scripts

**Assumptions:**
- GCP Organization exists with billing accounts configured
- GitHub Enterprise is available with capability to run self-hosted runners
- Customers are onboarded via an internal operations workflow (PRs to config repo)
- All products are containerized (Docker) and published to Artifact Registry; Instance Groups use golden images built via Packer
- Product teams own application-level Helm charts and application code; platform team owns base infrastructure and shared modules
- DNS is managed via Cloud DNS
- No hybrid cloud or on-premises connectivity required at this stage
- Application code implements tenant context handling (middleware, ORM scoping) for shared data access
- Default data sharing policy: "nonprod-can-read-prod" (configurable per customer)
- All applications expose health check endpoints (/healthz/ready, /healthz/live)
- Product teams follow 12-factor app principles
- Database schema includes standard authentication tables (users, sessions, tokens) that require sanitization during cloning

**Non-Goals:**
- Multi-cloud or hybrid cloud support
- Customer self-service onboarding portal (future roadmap)
- Real-time data streaming or event-driven architecture design
- FinOps dashboards and cost allocation tooling (phase 2)
- Migration of existing workloads (this is greenfield)
- Custom service mesh (using managed Istio)
- On-premises connectivity or VPN to customer networks
- Custom ETL pipelines for data cloning (using GCP-native services)

**Success Criteria:**
- New customer with 4 environments provisioned in < 30 minutes via automation
- New product deployed to an existing customer in < 15 minutes
- Zero manual steps for deploying a product version update to any customer
- Zero-downtime deployments with automated rollback on failure
- Tenant isolation validated: No cross-customer data or network access possible
- Cross-environment data sharing works within customer boundary
- Data cloning completes in < 30 minutes with verified sanitization
- All infrastructure changes auditable, peer-reviewed, and version-controlled
- Platform architecture supports 500+ customers without redesign
- Clear separation of responsibilities between platform team and product teams
- Infrastructure cost per customer decreases as customer count grows (economies of scale)
- Cluster upgrades validated in non-prod before production rollout
- Prod environment achieves 99.9% availability SLA (99.95% for enterprise tier)
- RTO/RPO targets met for each environment tier
- All security controls pass SOC 2 Type II audit
- Zero critical/high vulnerabilities in production container images
- No production credentials exposed in non-prod environments

**Stakeholders:**
- Platform Engineering: Owns Terraform modules, base infrastructure, CI/CD pipelines, ArgoCD setup, Istio configuration, shared Helm charts, data cloning automation
- Product Teams: Deploy their applications, manage product-level Helm values and feature configs, implement health checks and graceful shutdown
- Customer Success / Operations: Triggers customer onboarding via PR, manages customer-specific settings and customizations
- Security & Compliance: Reviews IAM policies, network segmentation, audit logs, access controls, VPC Service Controls, Binary Authorization policies, data sanitization procedures
- SRE: Monitors infrastructure health, manages incidents, handles autoscaling and capacity planning, owns DR procedures
- Finance: Requires cost visibility and allocation by customer, environment, and product

**Risks & Concerns:**
- Terraform state management at scale: 200+ customers with multiple environments
- Helm values sprawl: Managing customer-specific overrides across hundreds of deployments
- Cost explosion: Risk of over-provisioning if shared resources are not used effectively
- Blast radius: Impact if shared GKE cluster experiences failure (mitigated by env separation)
- Complexity of RBAC: Managing IAM and Kubernetes RBAC across projects and namespaces
- Application-level isolation risk: Bug in tenant context = data leak (mitigated by infra guardrails)
- Drift detection: Ensuring deployed infrastructure matches Terraform state
- Onboarding bottleneck: If automation is incomplete, customer onboarding becomes manual burden
- Version matrix complexity: Tracking which customer runs which product version
- Cross-environment data sharing: Must work seamlessly without compromising inter-customer isolation
- Service mesh overhead: Istio sidecar resource consumption and complexity
- Canary deployment complexity: Requires proper metric collection and analysis templates
- Multi-region complexity: Data replication lag, consistency challenges for enterprise tier
- Binary Authorization friction: May slow down emergency deployments (break-glass procedure needed)
- Data sanitization failure: If sanitization fails, production credentials could be exposed in dev (mitigated by verification step and blocking access until sanitization confirmed)
- Clone instance costs: Weekly clones create additional Cloud SQL instances (mitigated by using smaller tier for dev, auto-delete after retention period)

