10. Zero Trust Architecture:
    - Principle: Never trust, always verify; assume breach; least privilege
    - Layer 1 - Identity & Access:
      - Workload Identity: GKE pods authenticate to GCP services via federated identity (no static keys)
      - Service accounts: Minimal permissions, namespace-scoped
      - Short-lived tokens: 1-hour maximum lifetime
      - IAM Conditions: Context-aware access (IP, time, resource attributes)
      - No shared credentials between customers or environments
    - Layer 2 - Network Security:
      - mTLS everywhere (Istio service mesh, STRICT mode)
      - NetworkPolicy: Default deny all ingress/egress, explicit allow rules
      - VPC Service Controls: Data perimeter around sensitive projects
      - Private GKE clusters: No public API endpoint
      - Private Google Access: Access Google APIs without public IPs
      - Egress restricted: Allowlist-only external access
    - Layer 3 - Device & Workload Attestation:
      - Binary Authorization: Only signed container images can deploy
      - Image signing: CI/CD signs images with KMS-managed keys
      - Artifact Registry vulnerability scanning: Block CRITICAL/HIGH vulnerabilities
      - GKE Shielded Nodes: Secure boot, vTPM, integrity monitoring
      - Pod Security Standards: Restricted policy enforced on all namespaces
    - Layer 4 - Data Protection:
      - Encryption at rest: Customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) for Cloud SQL, GCS, BigQuery
      - Encryption in transit: TLS 1.2+ for all connections
      - VPC Service Controls: Prevent data exfiltration
      - Data Loss Prevention (DLP): Scan for sensitive data, automated redaction
      - Column-level encryption: Application-level encryption for PII fields
    - Layer 5 - Continuous Verification & Monitoring:
      - Security Command Center: Threat detection, vulnerability management
      - Cloud Audit Logs: All API calls logged, exported to BigQuery/SIEM
      - Falco: Runtime security monitoring for anomalous container behavior
      - Real-time alerting: PagerDuty/Slack integration for security events
      - Automated remediation: Cloud Functions for auto-response to threats
      - Regular penetration testing and security audits

11. Cross-Environment Data Sharing & Cloning (GCP-Native):
    
    Data Sharing Types:
    - Type 1 - Shared Reference Data: Static data (catalogs, configs, feature flags) in shared schema/prefix, all envs READ directly, no copying needed
    - Type 2 - Cross-Env Read Access: Dev/stage/uat can query prod data read-only via PostgreSQL GRANTs, BigQuery row policies, GCS IAM conditions
    - Type 3 - Data Cloning: Scheduled copy of prod transactional data to lower envs with mandatory sanitization
    
    GCP-Native Cloning Methods (No Custom ETL Scripts):
    
    Cloud SQL:
    - Primary Method: Cloud SQL Clone Instance (gcloud sql instances clone)
      - Copy-on-write at storage layer (instant, regardless of size)
      - Point-in-time clone supported
      - Followed by mandatory post-clone sanitization
    - Alternative: Read Replica for real-time read access (no write capability)
    - Automation: Cloud Scheduler → Cloud Function → Clone + Sanitize
    
    BigQuery:
    - Primary Method: Authorized Views with built-in anonymization
      - Views query prod tables at runtime
      - Anonymization transforms applied in view definition (CONCAT, mask functions)
      - No data copy, no storage cost, always up-to-date
    - Alternative: Scheduled Queries for materialized copies
      - Daily scheduled query with INSERT OVERWRITE
      - Transform/anonymize during copy
      - Use when materialized tables needed for performance
    
    GCS:
    - Primary Method: Same bucket with prefix-based IAM (no copy needed)
      - Prefixes: {customer}/shared/, {customer}/dev/, {customer}/prod/
      - IAM conditions control access per prefix
    - Alternative: Storage Transfer Service for scheduled sync
      - Exclude sensitive file prefixes
      - Incremental sync (only changed files)
    
    Network Path (Critical Security):
    - Clone operations happen at GCP STORAGE LAYER, not network layer
    - Data NEVER traverses customer VPC during clone
    - Data NEVER traverses internet
    - All data encrypted at rest and in transit by Google
    - Only post-clone sanitization uses VPC (private IP connection via Cloud SQL Connector)
    
    Network Path by Operation:
    | Operation                  | Network Path              | VPC Traffic | Internet |
    |----------------------------|---------------------------|-------------|----------|
    | Cloud SQL Clone            | GCP Storage Layer         | None        | None     |
    | Cloud SQL Read Replica     | Google Internal Repl.     | None        | None     |
    | Post-Clone Sanitization    | VPC (Private IP)          | Yes         | None     |
    | BigQuery Views             | BigQuery Internal         | None        | None     |
    | BigQuery Scheduled Query   | BigQuery Internal         | None        | None     |
    | GCS Storage Transfer       | GCS Internal              | None        | None     |
    
    Mandatory Post-Clone Sanitization (Security Critical):
    
    Data that MUST be sanitized after clone:
    - Authentication Credentials:
      - password_hash → Replace with known test password hash
      - password_salt → Regenerate or NULL
      - mfa_secret → NULL (disable MFA)
      - mfa_backup_codes → NULL
      - recovery_key → NULL
      - security_questions → NULL
    
    - Tokens & Sessions (TRUNCATE entire tables):
      - sessions
      - refresh_tokens
      - access_tokens
      - oauth_tokens
      - remember_tokens
      - password_reset_tokens
      - email_verification_tokens
    
    - API & Integration Credentials:
      - api_keys → Replace with TEST_ prefixed keys
      - webhook_secrets → Regenerate
      - oauth_client_secrets → Regenerate
      - third_party_tokens → NULL
    
    - Payment Data:
      - payment_method_tokens → NULL
      - card_fingerprints → NULL
      - stripe_customer_id → Replace with test IDs
    
    - PII Anonymization:
      - email → user_{id}@test.local
      - phone → 555-000-{id}
      - name → TestUser{id}
      - address → {id} Test Street, Test City, TS 00000
      - ssn → XXX-XX-{last4}
    
    Post-Clone Verification (Automated):
    - Verify all password hashes replaced
    - Verify MFA disabled for all users
    - Verify session tables empty
    - Verify API keys have TEST_ prefix
    - Add _clone_metadata table with sanitization marker
    
    Test Credentials (For Dev Environment):
    - All users password: TestPassword123!
    - Admin account: admin@test.local / TestPassword123!
    - MFA: Disabled for all users
    - API Keys: Prefixed with TEST_
    
    Automation Workflow:
    1. Cloud Scheduler triggers weekly (Sunday 2 AM)
    2. Cloud Function initiates clone operation
    3. Wait for clone completion (1-5 minutes)
    4. Cloud Function connects via Private IP + Cloud SQL Connector
    5. Execute sanitization SQL script
    6. Verify sanitization completed
    7. Mark clone ready for use
    8. Alert on failure
    
    VPC Service Controls (Additional Protection):
    - Wrap data projects in VPC-SC perimeter
    - Clone operations allowed within perimeter
    - Block any data export outside perimeter
    - Block access from unauthorized networks/identities

12. Multi-Product Infrastructure Provisioning:
    
    Overview:
    - Each product in the catalog defines its infrastructure requirements
    - Customers select which products they need
    - Infrastructure is dynamically provisioned based on product selection
    - Day 2 operations: Add/remove products triggers infrastructure changes
    - No unused infrastructure provisioned (cost optimization)
    
    Product Catalog Structure:
    - Platform maintains a catalog of 15-20 products
    - Each product has a definition file (product.yaml) specifying:
      - Runtime type (GKE, Cloud Run, Instance Group)
      - Infrastructure requirements (databases, caches, queues, storage, etc.)
      - Resource sizing per environment (dev/stage/uat/prod)
      - Dependencies on other products
      - Networking requirements (ingress, egress allowlist)
      - Compliance requirements (PCI, encryption, etc.)
      - Cost metadata for estimation
    
    Product Definition Schema:
    ```yaml
    apiVersion: platform.company.io/v1
    kind: ProductDefinition
    metadata:
      name: {product-name}
      labels:
        category: core|analytics|integration|utility
        tier: standard|premium
    spec:
      displayName: "Human Readable Name"
      description: "Product description"
      version: "x.y.z"
      
      runtime:
        type: gke|cloud-run|instance-group
        # Runtime-specific configuration
        
      infrastructure:
        cloudSql:
          required: true|false
          type: postgresql|mysql
          sizing:
            dev: {tier, diskSize}
            prod: {tier, diskSize, ha, backup}
        redis:
          required: true|false
          sizing: {per-environment}
        bigQuery:
          required: true|false
          datasets: [{name, tables}]
        storage:
          required: true|false
          buckets: [{name, purpose, lifecycle}]
        pubsub:
          required: true|false
          topics: [{name, subscriptions}]
        kms:
          required: true|false
          keyRings: [{name, keys}]
        cloudTasks:
          required: true|false
          queues: [{name, config}]
        cloudScheduler:
          required: true|false
          jobs: [{name, schedule}]
        secrets:
          required: true|false
          secrets: [{name, description}]
          
      networking:
        ingress:
          enabled: true|false
          public: true|false
          path: "/api/..."
        egress:
          allowedDestinations: [external-apis]
          
      dependencies:
        required: [product-names]
        optional: [product-names]
        
      compliance:
        pciDss: true|false
        dataClassification: sensitive|internal|public
